c0fb32e62b6effe133c527fee528c47b6f1c8dc7dd1d77d7d04f42b4c3d2b.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the quadrilateral shown above under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "53c5c0e972e801f66ce79035431092eeadb57bbe23434bd1e133d6aa4caefee5"} {"question" "q-162200" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $T$ is the origin." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "53c5c0e972e801f66ce79035431092eeadb57bbe23434bd1e133d6aa4caefee5"} {"question" "q-162200" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3117cac283fb94cc19d5fe97a6acc1c3e0354b103cbb0237db3b15f187694112"} {"question" "q-162200" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "53c5c0e972e801f66ce79035431092eeadb57bbe23434bd1e133d6aa4caefee5"} {"question" "q-162200" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A line segment"] ["text" "A point, different from the origin"] ["text" "A quadrilateral"] ["text" "A triangle"] ["text" "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "53c5c0e972e801f66ce79035431092eeadb57bbe23434bd1e133d6aa4caefee5"} {"question" "q-162200" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "53c5c0e972e801f66ce79035431092eeadb57bbe23434bd1e133d6aa4caefee5"} {"question" "q-162206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/e6d0ce147aec90c3840a893e36596fbadf981ff96559f65d055f4486406cca7f.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the quadrilateral shown above under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "53c5c0e972e801f66ce79035431092eeadb57bbe23434bd1e133d6aa4caefee5"} {"question" "q-162206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a nonsingular matrix. Therefore, the image of our object under the action of  $T$ is a quadrilateral." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "53c5c0e972e801f66ce79035431092eeadb57bbe23434bd1e133d6aa4caefee5"} {"question" "q-162206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3117cac283fb94cc19d5fe97a6acc1c3e0354b103cbb0237db3b15f187694112"} {"question" "q-162206" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "53c5c0e972e801f66ce79035431092eeadb57bbe23434bd1e133d6aa4caefee5"} {"question" "q-162206" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A line segment"] ["text" "A point, different from the origin"] ["text" "A quadrilateral"] ["text" "A triangle"] ["text" "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system"]] "categ