file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "53c5c0e972e801f66ce79035431092eeadb57bbe23434bd1e133d6aa4caefee5"} {"question" "q-162199" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A line segment"] ["text" "A pentagon"] ["text" "A point, different from the origin"] ["text" "A rectangle"] ["text" "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "53c5c0e972e801f66ce79035431092eeadb57bbe23434bd1e133d6aa4caefee5"} {"question" "q-162199" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "53c5c0e972e801f66ce79035431092eeadb57bbe23434bd1e133d6aa4caefee5"} {"question" "q-162145" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/e2ab6906100a96a123b4b1520613f1e38fe2c7e77af390a8efe0596a2861727d.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "53c5c0e972e801f66ce79035431092eeadb57bbe23434bd1e133d6aa4caefee5"} {"question" "q-162145" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-3)-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. Therefore, the image of the triangle under the action of $T$ is a line segment." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "53c5c0e972e801f66ce79035431092eeadb57bbe23434bd1e133d6aa4caefee5"} {"question" "q-162145" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3117cac283fb94cc19d5fe97a6acc1c3e0354b103cbb0237db3b15f187694112"} {"question" "q-162145" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "53c5c0e972e801f66ce79035431092eeadb57bbe23434bd1e133d6aa4caefee5"} {"question" "q-162145" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A line segment"] ["text" "A point, different from the origin"] ["text" "A rectangle"] ["text" "A triangle"] ["text" "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "53c5c0e972e801f66ce79035431092eeadb57bbe23434bd1e133d6aa4caefee5"} {"question" "q-162145" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A line segment"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "53c5c0e972e801f66ce79035431092eeadb57bbe23434bd1e133d6aa4caefee5"} {"question" "e-12423" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,2), (0,1)$ and $(1,1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S',$ the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf T?$\n\n- $(-2,0)$\n\n- $(0,0)$\n\n- $(2,0)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/example-12423.json" "file_sha256" "b47ae757f74c19467edaf19071d8ea9d8df05f6c4559a55bdf0ceb86ccfadf8c"} {"question" "e-12423" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $S'$ are $(-2,0),$ $(0,0),$ and $(2,0).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II,  and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/example-12423.json" "file_sha256" "b47ae757f74c19467edaf19071d8ea9d8df05f6c4559a55bdf0ceb86ccfadf8c"} {"question" "e-12424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n  Find the image of the triangle shown below under the action of $\\mathbf T.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/853fd7d5c09944a5bacd0c2b3453504f3fa1eea9d8d3b2c3bdd4f0f44889dc16.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/example-12424.json" "file_sha256" "155c5691fe02e425bc73640f7ce08367c0a0042428fd13851f92507c0a237579"} {"question" "e-12424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(3,-6), (1,-2),$ and $(-2,4).$  Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/3fd1073b2eaa5aa9c21e537fcc4dc6cf538ab2992c0732f64da50dbcb63d8fd4.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/example-12424.json" "file_sha256" "155c5691fe02e425bc73640f7ce08367c0a0042428fd13851f92507c0a237579"} {"question" "e-12438" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/89efcd0ecb51dac08a1102c5a347eea3459f7209de7cbac0a88b192259ca3b7e.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $\\mathbf{T}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/example-12438.json" "file_sha256" "bc6ec9a9a8eb97e889b4786ed77c98111a6db2ed94dd2ca7f39ed45f31ca9c97"} {"question" "e-12438" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with associated $2 \\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., $\\det(T) \\neq 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., $\\det(T) = 0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the origin." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/example-12438.json" "file_sha256" "bc6ec9a9a8eb97e889b4786ed77c98111a6db2ed94dd2ca7f39ed45f31ca9c97"}] "usage" {"e-12423" [] "e-12438" [] "q-162145" [] "q-162206" [] "e-12424" [] "q-21061" [] "q-21067" []} "basis" 1049 "retractions" [[17592186106082 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,-1), (0,-1)$, and $(0, 0)$. Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S^{′}$, the image of $S$ under the action of $T$?\n\n1. $(4,-1)$\n2. $(0, 0)$\n3. $(-4,-8)$"] [17592186106081 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/q-21061.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T$?"] [17592186105994 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/q-162206.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhat would be the image of the quadrilateral shown above under the action of $T$?"] [17592186105992 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/q-162145.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T$?"] [17592186105383 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,2), (0,1)$ and $(1,1)$. Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S'$, the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$?\n\n1. $(-2,0)$\n2. $(0,0)$\n3. $(2,0)$"] [17592186105383 :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nTX &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $S'$ are $(-2,0)$, $(0,0)$, and $(2,0)$.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [17592186105384 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ Find the image of the triangle shown below under the action of $\\mathbf T$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/71b4c00c3f3f2354e52c4ddadc41fdd1.png)"] [17592186105384 :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nTX &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(3,-6), (1,-2)$, and $(-2,4)$. Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/90a937e06df1fa3177ee4608a88110e5.png)"] [17592186105385 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/66b3a70a542c03540cf219bdce0d024d.png)"] [17592186105385 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with associated $2 \\times 2$ matrix $T$, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., $\\det(T) \\neq 0$, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., $\\det(T) = 0$, we have two additional subcases:If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0)$, the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the origin."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186102200 17592186102201 17592186102202 17592186102203 17592186102204 17592186102212 17592186102213 17592186102214 17592186102215 17592186102216 17592186102724 17592186102725 17592186102726 17592186102727 17592186102728 17592186102730 17592186102731 17592186102732 17592186102733 17592186102734] "immutable_fields" [[17592186102199 :answer-field/correct] [17592186102199 :answer-field/id] [17592186102199 :answer-field/key] [17592186102199 :answer-field/type] [17592186102211 :answer-field/correct] [17592186102211 :answer-field/id] [17592186102211 :answer-field/key] [17592186102211 :answer-field/type] [17592186102723 :answer-field/correct] [17592186102723 :answer-field/id] [17592186102723 :answer-field/key] [17592186102723 :answer-field/type] [17592186102729 :answer-field/correct] [17592186102729 :answer-field/id] [17592186102729 :answer-field/key] [17592186102729 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
